    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The cost of living in Houston is below the national urban average, mainly because housing costs much less than in other big metros. The Greater Houston Partnership, citing the C2ER Cost of Living Index for Q2 2026, puts Houston's overall living costs 6.3% below the national urban average and its housing costs 24.8% below it. The catch is that property taxes, insurance, summer electricity and car costs are higher than many newcomers expect.</p>
<p>This guide walks through each major expense, what drives it in Houston, and how to estimate your own number. It ends with an illustrative monthly budget you can adapt.</p>

<h2>Houston Cost of Living at a Glance</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Expense</th><th>How Houston compares</th><th>What drives it</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Housing</td><td>Lower than most large U.S. metros</td><td>Abundant land, lots of new construction, wide price range</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property taxes</td><td>Higher than many states</td><td>No state income tax; local units fund services through property tax</td></tr>
<tr><td>Home and flood insurance</td><td>Often higher than expected</td><td>Hurricane, wind, hail and flood exposure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electricity</td><td>High in summer</td><td>Long air-conditioning season; plan choice matters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transportation</td><td>Significant</td><td>Car-dependent region, long commutes, toll roads</td></tr>
<tr><td>State income tax</td><td>None</td><td>Texas does not tax personal income</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Housing: Buying a Home</h2>
<p>Housing is where Houston stands out. The Houston Association of Realtors (HAR) reported a median single-family sale price of about $325,000 for the Greater Houston area in September 2026, essentially flat from a year earlier. That figure covers the whole region, from inner-Loop neighborhoods to far-out suburbs, so your target area may be well above or below it.</p>
<p>What the median hides:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Location premium:</strong> Inside the Loop, close-in neighborhoods cost more per square foot and often mean smaller lots or townhomes. See our guide to the <a href="/blog/best-neighborhoods-in-houston/">best neighborhoods in Houston</a>.</li>
<li><strong>New construction:</strong> Suburban master-planned communities offer newer, larger homes, but often add HOA dues and MUD taxes.</li>
<li><strong>Carrying costs:</strong> In Houston, taxes and insurance can add a large amount on top of principal and interest, so compare full monthly payments, not just prices.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before you shop, run your income and debts through our <a href="/tools/home-affordability-calculator/">home affordability calculator</a> with realistic tax and insurance estimates included.</p>

<h2>Housing: Renting in Houston</h2>
<p>Renting is common, especially for newcomers who want to learn the city first. Options range from large apartment complexes along the freeways to garage apartments in older neighborhoods and single-family homes for lease in the suburbs. Rents follow the same pattern as prices: higher in close-in, walkable areas and lower farther out.</p>
<p>Renters skip property taxes, homeowners insurance and big repair bills, but still need renters insurance and, in flood-prone areas, should consider contents flood coverage. Ask landlords about average utility costs, parking fees and any flood history before you sign. If you are weighing renting against buying, our <a href="/tools/rent-vs-buy-calculator/">rent vs. buy calculator</a> compares the two over time.</p>

<h2>Property Taxes: The Big Trade-Off</h2>
<p>Texas has no state property tax, but cities, counties, school districts, the flood control district, the hospital district, community colleges and special districts each levy their own. For a home inside the City of Houston and Houston ISD with no MUD, the main 2025 adopted rates add up to roughly $2.13 per $100 of value before exemptions.</p>
<p>Exemptions bring the effective rate down. Homesteads get at least $140,000 off for school taxes, and Harris County offers a 20% optional homestead exemption. Homes in MUDs pay an additional district tax. For the full breakdown and a worked example, read our guide to the <a href="/blog/houston-property-tax-rate/">Houston property tax rate</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Apply for your homestead exemption as soon as you own and live in the home.</li>
<li>Protest your appraised value each spring if comparable sales support a lower number.</li>
<li>Ask for the full list of taxing units on any home you consider.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Homeowners and Flood Insurance</h2>
<p>Insurance is one of the most variable costs in Houston. Wind, hail and hurricane exposure all affect homeowners premiums, and many policies carry a separate wind or hurricane deductible set as a percentage of dwelling coverage. Roof age, construction type and claims history matter too.</p>
<p>Standard homeowners policies do not cover flood. Flood coverage comes from the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or a private insurer, and NFIP policies generally have a 30-day waiting period. Hurricane Harvey in 2017 flooded many homes outside mapped high-risk zones, so get a flood quote even if your lender does not require one.</p>
<p>Get insurance quotes during your option period, before you are locked into a purchase.</p>

<h2>Electricity, Water and Other Utilities</h2>
<p>Electricity is the utility Houston newcomers notice most. Summers are long and humid, so air conditioning runs for months and summer bills can be several times higher than spring bills.</p>
<p>Most of the Houston area is in Texas's competitive retail electricity market. CenterPoint Energy owns the wires and handles outages, but you choose a retail electric provider and plan. The Public Utility Commission of Texas runs PowerToChoose.org to compare plans.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Read the Electricity Facts Label:</strong> Check the price at your actual usage, any minimum-usage fees and the early termination fee.</li>
<li><strong>Track your contract end date:</strong> Expired fixed-rate plans can roll onto pricier variable rates.</li>
<li><strong>Cut cooling costs:</strong> Change HVAC filters regularly, seal ducts and windows, and use a programmable or smart thermostat.</li>
</ul>
<p>[[product:nest-thermostat]]</p>
<p>Water, sewer and trash usually come from the city or your MUD. Natural gas, internet and mobile service round out the list.</p>

<h2>Transportation, Tolls and Car Dependence</h2>
<p>For most households, transportation is the second-biggest cost after housing. Houston is spread out and built around freeways, and outside a few inner-Loop neighborhoods most errands require a car. Many households need two vehicles.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Car payments and insurance:</strong> Budget for both, and shop insurance after you move, since rates depend on your ZIP code.</li>
<li><strong>Fuel:</strong> Long commutes add up quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Tolls:</strong> The Sam Houston Tollway, Westpark Tollway, Hardy Toll Road and other tolled routes save time but cost money. TxDOT notes that drivers with a prepaid toll tag account pay the lowest toll rates.</li>
<li><strong>Transit:</strong> METRO runs buses and light rail, which can replace a car for some downtown, Midtown, Medical Center and Museum District commutes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Living closer to work can cost more in rent or price but save on fuel, tolls and time. Test your commute at rush hour before you choose an address.</p>

<h2>Groceries, Sales Tax and Everyday Spending</h2>
<p>Texas charges a 6.25% state sales tax, and local governments can add up to 2%. Inside the City of Houston the combined rate is 8.25%: the state rate plus 1% for the city and 1% for METRO. Most grocery food bought to prepare at home is exempt from sales tax, while restaurant and prepared food is taxable.</p>
<p>Houston has plenty of grocery competition, from H-E-B and Kroger to warehouse clubs and international markets, which helps families shop around. Dining out is a big part of local life, and that spending is easy to underestimate.</p>

<h2>Childcare and Family Costs</h2>
<p>For families with young children, childcare can rival a mortgage payment. Costs vary widely by provider type (center, in-home or nanny), age of the child and neighborhood. Infant care is usually the most expensive. Get quotes from several providers near home and near work before you choose where to live, and ask about waitlists.</p>
<p>Public schools are funded partly through property taxes, which is one reason families compare school districts and tax rates together. Compare districts by address, since attendance zones can split neighborhoods.</p>

<h2>No State Income Tax: How Much Does It Help?</h2>
<p>Texas does not tax individual personal income, and voters added an explicit ban to the state constitution in 2019. For higher earners moving from high-income-tax states, this can offset much of the extra property tax and insurance cost. For renters and lower-income households, the savings can be even more meaningful because they pay property tax only indirectly through rent.</p>
<p>The real comparison is total tax: sales tax plus property tax in Texas versus income, sales and property taxes where you live now. A CPA can run that comparison for your situation.</p>

